#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# modular_inverse

#% text
# `modular_inverse(k, n)` gives the `r` with `mod(k*r, n) == 1`, in the range `0 .. n - 1`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The inverse exists when `gcd(k, n) == 1`; otherwise the result stays unevaluated, as `power_mod(k, -1, n)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
modular_inverse(3, 7)
#% output
#= 5

#% cell
modular_inverse(35, 97)
#% output
#= 61

#% cell
modular_inverse(-2, 9)
#% output
#= 4

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The product is 1 modulo `n`:

#% cell
mod(17*modular_inverse(17, 1000), 1000)
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# No inverse when the numbers share a factor:

#% cell
modular_inverse(4, 10)
#% output
#= power_mod(4, -1, 10)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `power_mod`, `extended_gcd`, `mod`
